chore(ci): one runner per issue event, not two

An `issues: opened` event started two workflows, `secret-redact` and
`needs-type`. Each does about ten seconds of work, and GitHub bills a
started job a full minute, so typing an issue cost two minutes to do
twenty seconds — 161 of the 850 minutes measured on 2026-09-19 (#232).

They are one workflow now, and one job: the same two steps, the same
scripts, and the union of the triggers they had, each step conditioned
on the events its guard needs. A `labeled` event still only types, an
`issue_comment` still only redacts, and each step says what it did in
the summary, so a run still names the guard that acted.

One condition widened rather than moved: an `issues: edited` event now
runs the typing guard too, which `needs-type.yml` did not subscribe to.
It is free, because the runner is already up for the redaction guard,
and idempotent, because the guard writes only when the labels disagree
with it. The header says so rather than leaving it to be found.

The workflow is called `issue-guards`, because a workflow that redacts
credentials *and* applies type labels cannot be named after one of
them. The file stays `secret-redact.yml` though, and the header there
says why: `workflow_dispatch` resolves a workflow by its path on the
default branch, so renaming the file would 404 every dispatch until
the merge — this fold would land unproven, which is how #203, #207 and
#209 each shipped. The path is a cheap commit for whoever wants it
once this is on main.

The dispatch path gains a `guard` input (both/redact/type) so either
guard can still be exercised on its own from a branch (#209). The
`needs-type` label, the note and the scripts keep their names; the
sentinel's watch-list and ADR-0014's parenthetical now name the
workflow that exists.

Part of #232

 — Iris · Frontend Engineer

